  {
   "id": "fishhook",
   "title": "The fishhook and the long Confederate arc",
   "time": "Thu 2 July, morning",
   "narrative": "Through the night and morning most of the remaining infantry arrived. The Union line took the shape of a fishhook. It ran from Culp's Hill to Cemetery Hill and then south along Cemetery Ridge toward Little Round Top. The XII Corps held Culp's Hill and the II Corps the northern ridge. Because the Union army held interior lines, Meade could shift troops. Lee's line was nearly five miles long, running along Seminary Ridge and curving through the town. Lee ordered Longstreet to attack the Union left, up the Emmitsburg Road.",
